.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_PAUSE 3 "2026-07-18" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_pause \- temporarily stop delivering events for an fd registration, without destroying it
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_event_loop_pause(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function works on an fd only: a call on a registration that comes
from a queue selectable or a ccol_channel selectable returns
ccol_invalid_args, the same restriction that ccol_event_loop_modify
has.

ccol_event_loop_remove fully unregisters reg and defers it for a later
free, while ccol_event_loop_pause leaves reg fully intact: reg keeps
its slot on the shared epoll entry of the fd below it, keeps counting
toward ccol_event_loop_reg_count, and keeps the same
ccol_event_loop_reg_generation.

This function recomputes the combined epoll interest mask of the fd so
that the mask excludes reg. If the fd carries another direction that is
itself live and not paused, this is a single EPOLL_CTL_MOD call. If,
after the pause, every direction on the fd is paused, the library
removes the fd from the epoll interest set of the kernel completely, so
the fd produces no wake-up of any kind for as long as it stays fully
paused, not even for its own error condition and its own hangup
condition, which the kernel reports whatever interest mask you ask for.
No on_readable, on_writable or on_error callback fires for reg while it
is paused. The other direction on the same fd, if there is one, is
unaffected.

This call is the cheap alternative to a ccol_event_loop_remove and a
later ccol_event_loop_add, for a caller pattern where the same logical
registration comes back: for example, a connection that goes to a
worker thread for blocking body I/O and then comes back to the reactor
for its next request. A fresh ccol_event_loop_add and
ccol_event_loop_remove pair costs one heap allocation and one free,
plus churn in the chmap of the fd registry, and pause and resume avoid
both completely.

With more than one reactor thread (num_reactor_threads > 1),
EPOLLONESHOT is in play, and a dispatch worker runs its own re-arm one
more time after a paused callback returns. This causes no problem:
since a re-arm always calculates the epoll mask from the current
state, the extra re-arm applies the same mask, which does not include
reg, and it does not cause a race. In this case too, pause and resume
prevent the allocation and the changes to the registry.

A pause of a reg that is already paused succeeds and does nothing.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  ccol_event_loop the registration belongs to.
reg   Registration to pause.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        The function paused reg, or reg was already
                    paused.
ccol_invalid_args   loop is CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID, or a handle that
                    is stale, or one that something already destroyed;
                    or reg is CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID, or it fails to
                    resolve for another reason, such as a removal at
                    the same time; or reg is a registration for a
                    queue or for a ccol_channel.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call at the same time as ccol_event_loop_remove(), and from
inside a callback that runs on one of the reactor threads.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* Inside an on_readable callback, once a full set of request headers is
   parsed and just before the connection goes to a worker thread for its
   own direct, blocking body I/O. reg is the handle that the callback
   receives: */
conn->reg = reg;
ccol_event_loop_pause(loop, reg);
if (ctpool_submit(worker_pool, process_request, conn, NULL) != ccol_success)
    ccol_event_loop_resume(loop, reg);  /* no worker: the reactor keeps it */
/* ... later, on the worker thread, after the server sends the
   response, when the connection is ready for its next request: */
ccol_event_loop_resume(loop, conn->reg);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_resume (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_add (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_remove (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_modify (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_reg_generation (3)
